[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"LSP: Circle-Ellipse Dilemma and Handling Exceptions/Preconditions"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP) is the 'L' in the SOLID principles. Formulated by Barbara Liskov, it states that objects in a system should be replaceable with instances of their subtypes without altering the correctness of that system. While conceptually straightforward, LSP introduces subtle design traps when mapping real-world mathematics to object-oriented code."},{"order":3,"type":"heading","text":"The Classic Circle-Ellipse Dilemma"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"In mathematics, a circle is a special kind of ellipse where the semi-major axis equals the semi-minor axis (or radius). Naturally, developers often attempt to model this inheritance hierarchy in code, making Circle inherit from Ellipse."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"class Ellipse {
    protected double width;
    protected double height;

    public void setWidth(double width) { this.width = width; }
    public void setHeight(double height) { this.height = height; }
    public double getArea() { return Math.PI * width * height; }
}

class Circle extends Ellipse {
    @Override
    public void setWidth(double width) {
        this.width = width;
        this.height = width;
    }

    @Override
    public void setHeight(double height) {
        this.width = height;
        this.height = height;
    }
}"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"At first glance, this code looks harmless. However, it violates LSP. Consider a function designed to mutate an Ellipse and verify its dimensions:"},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"void resizeAndVerify(Ellipse ellipse) {
    ellipse.setWidth(5.0);
    ellipse.setHeight(4.0);
    assert(ellipse.getWidth() == 5.0 && ellipse.getHeight() == 4.0);
}"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"If you pass an instance of Circle to 'resizeAndVerify', the assertion will fail. Because a Circle forces width and height to remain equal, calling 'setHeight(4.0)' implicitly overwrites the width to 4.0. The subclass (Circle) has altered the expected behavior of the base class (Ellipse)."},{"order":9,"type":"heading","text":"Handling Preconditions and Exceptions under LSP"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"LSP strictly governs how subtypes must handle preconditions, postconditions, and exceptions. When designing inheritance hierarchies, remember these rules:"},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Preconditions cannot be strengthened in a subtype. If a base class method accepts any integer, the subclass method cannot throw an exception for negative integers."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Postconditions cannot be weakened in a subtype. The guarantees made by the base class must still be fulfilled by the subclass."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Exceptions thrown by a subtype must be historical or subtypes of the exceptions thrown by the base class. A subtype should not throw entirely new, unexpected exception types."},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"To fix the Circle-Ellipse dilemma, we must recognize that mutable Ellipses and Circles are fundamentally incompatible types in object-oriented programming. If shapes are immutable, inheritance can sometimes work. Alternatively, we should depend on a common interface rather than a mutable base class."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"interface Shape {
    double getArea();
}

class Ellipse implements Shape {
    private final double width;
    private final double height;

    public Ellipse(double width, double height) {
        this.width = width;
        this.height = height;
    }

    public double getArea() { return Math.PI * width * height; }
}

class Circle implements Shape {
    private final double radius;

    public Circle(double radius) {
        this.radius = radius;
    }

    public double getArea() { return Math.PI * radius * radius; }
}"},{"order":16,"type":"heading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Circle-Ellipse dilemma teaches us that real-world 'is-a' relationships do not always translate to valid object-oriented inheritance. By honoring the Liskov Substitution Principle, respecting method preconditions, and favoring composition or interfaces over mutable inheritance hierarchies, you can ensure your systems remain robust, predictable, and maintainable."}]
